e returned to the fore. Growth\n                                                                                                      1.4% in 2012, vs. 3.4% one year earlier.\n              forecasts point to a recession of 0.3% in 2012 and 0.5% in 2013.\n                                                                                                      In foreign trade, the overall balance of payments improved and is expected to come\n              In Japan                                                                                to CFAF 51 billion in 2012, down from CFAF 57 billion in 2011. The current account\n              Japan managed to emerge from recession thanks to post-tsunami reconstruction            deficit as a percentage of GDP is estimated at 10% for 2012, vs. 8% in 2011,\n              investments.                                                                            above the 5% community ceiling, due to a sharp increase in imports of food\n                                                                                                      and oil products.\n              In emerging markets\n                                                                                                      In public finances, fiscal management in 2012 featured healthy revenue and grants\n              Production is reportedly being slowed by the contraction in exports to the European\n                                                                                                      (+ 13%) and better control over public spending (+ 74%). As a percentage of GDP,\n              Union. However, activity remains still strong, driven by healthy domestic demand.\n                                                                                                      the public deficit came to 5.9% in 2012, down from 6.7% in 2011.\n              Growth forecasts for China come to 7.5% for 2012, and 8.6% for 2013.\n                                         